# 4. 🔢 Primitive Values

Primitive values include:

```text
number
string
boolean
undefined
null
symbol
bigint
```

Example:

```js
let age = 25;
let name = "John";
let isStudent = true;
```

Simplified view:

```text
STACK
┌────────────────────┐
│ age = 25           │
├────────────────────┤
│ name = "John"      │
├────────────────────┤
│ isStudent = true   │
└────────────────────┘
```

When you copy a primitive:

```js
let a = 10;
let b = a;

b = 20;

console.log(a); // 10
console.log(b); // 20
```

Conceptually:

```text
STACK

┌──────────────┐
│ a = 10       │
├──────────────┤
│ b = 10       │
└──────────────┘

        ↓

b = 20

┌──────────────┐
│ a = 10       │
├──────────────┤
│ b = 20       │
└──────────────┘

✅ a is NOT affected
```

### 🔑 Key point

> **Primitive assignment copies the value.**

---

# 5. 📦 Objects / Reference Values

Example:

```js
let user1 = {
    name: "John"
};

let user2 = user1;
```

Simplified model:

```text
              STACK                       HEAP

        ┌──────────────┐
        │ user1 ───────┼─────────────┐
        ├──────────────┤             │
        │ user2 ───────┼─────────┐   │
        └──────────────┘         │   │
                                 │   ↓
                                 │ ┌────────────────┐
                                 └→│ name: "John"   │
                                   └────────────────┘
```

Both variables refer to the **same object**.

Now:

```js
user2.name = "Bob";
```

Result:

```text
              STACK                       HEAP

        ┌──────────────┐
        │ user1 ───────┼─────────────┐
        ├──────────────┤             │
        │ user2 ───────┼─────────┐   │
        └──────────────┘         │   │
                                 │   ↓
                                 │ ┌────────────────┐
                                 └→│ name: "Bob"    │
                                   └────────────────┘
```

Therefore:

```js
console.log(user1.name); // "Bob"
console.log(user2.name); // "Bob"
```

### 🔑 Key point

> **Object assignment copies the reference, not the object itself.**

---

# 6. 🆚 Primitive vs Object

```text
              PRIMITIVE                         OBJECT
                 │                                │
                 ↓                                ↓
          ┌─────────────┐                 ┌──────────────┐
          │   value     │                 │   object     │
          │     10      │                 │ { name: ...} │
          └─────────────┘                 └──────────────┘
                 │                                ↑
                 │                                │
                 │                        reference
                 │                                │
          variable holds                  variable holds
             the value                     a reference
```

### Primitive

```js
let a = 10;
let b = a;

b = 20;
```

```text
a ──→ 10
b ──→ 20

Different values
```

### Object

```js
let a = { x: 10 };
let b = a;

b.x = 20;
```

```text
a ──────────┐
            ├────→ { x: 20 }
b ──────────┘

Same object
```

